The Local Government Association is fighting to maintain its position after suffering two major setbacks in less than a week.
Last week the Department for Communities and Local Government announced an immediate £2m cut in funding for the membership body, just days before Sir Merrick Cockell, the association's chair, publicly warned communities secretary Eric Pickles that a lack of certainty about public funding would lead to "firesale decisions".
Now England's eight core cities – local authorities that are major drivers of the national economy and have courted central government to win lucrative "city deals" – have served notice of their intention to quit the association.
In a joint statement, the cities of Birmingham, Bristol, Leeds, Liverpool, Manchester, Newcastle, Nottingham and Sheffield said: "Whilst we recognise that there are matters which affect a large number of local authorities in a similar way, there are a range of critical issues which affect large urban areas differently, and on which we need to have more focused representation at the national level."
The cities are already represented by a joint lobby group, Core Cities, which has achieved huge successes in a short period. The statement said that each of the eight cities had served 12 months notice of their intention to leave the LGA, but that the group was still open to discussion about the future of the association.
"We are looking for a positive way forward that will meet all our needs and take best advantage of this important moment for our cities, their businesses and communities. Core Cities have therefore invited the LGA to participate in discussions to explore options for our future relationship."
